# Dagric OS — User applications
# The rule: ship what a normal person needs on day one, nothing else.
# Everything beyond this comes from Discover (Flatpak), chosen BY the user.

# Web
firefox-esr

# Office — the three components people actually use, not the full suite
libreoffice-writer
libreoffice-calc
libreoffice-impress
libreoffice-kf6

# THE USER INTERFACE OF THESE TWO, IN THE LANGUAGES THIS OS ALREADY SPEAKS.
#
# The dictionaries below were the second half of this problem, not the whole of
# it, and fixing them made the remaining half look finished. Read the Aug-4 Pro
# ISO and the truth was: 544 German .mo catalogues on the image, plasmashell,
# dolphin, konsole and systemsettings among them, so the DESKTOP is German —
# and /usr/lib/libreoffice/share/registry/ held exactly one langpack file,
# Langpack-en-US.xcd, with no locale packs under firefox-esr at all.
#
# So a German owner got a German desktop, German Dagric tools, and German red
# underlines inside an English Writer. That is the worst of the three possible
# states: fully English would at least be consistent, and fully German would be
# right. Half-translated reads as broken, and it reads that way in Writer and
# Firefox specifically — the two applications this audience spends its day in.
#
# These are Suggests, not Recommends, so 01dagric-norecommends is not the cause
# here and removing it would not have helped; apt never installs Suggests. They
# have to be named.
#
# NAMES CHECKED WITH `apt-get install -s`, NOT `apt-cache show`. Spanish has no
# firefox-esr-l10n-es or thunderbird-l10n-es — only es-es, es-ar, es-cl, es-mx —
# while LibreOffice does use a plain -es. Guessing a symmetric set would have
# put two nonexistent names in this file and rolled the build back an hour in,
# which is exactly how signon-ui went. apt-cache show is NOT the test: it exits
# 0 with empty output for a name that only appears as a dependency reference,
# so it calls signon-ui present. install -s calls it absent, correctly.
#
# 12.6 MB for both editions. The l10n packages are UI strings only; offline
# help is a separate libreoffice-help-* set and is deliberately not taken.
firefox-esr-l10n-de
firefox-esr-l10n-es-es
firefox-esr-l10n-fr
firefox-esr-l10n-it
firefox-esr-l10n-pt-br
libreoffice-l10n-de
libreoffice-l10n-es
libreoffice-l10n-fr
libreoffice-l10n-it
libreoffice-l10n-pt-br

# AND QT'S OWN STRINGS, which none of the above covers. Measured on a running
# installed machine: /usr/share/qt6/translations holds qtwebengine_locales and
# NOTHING ELSE — zero qtbase_*.qm files. So on a German, French, Spanish,
# Italian or Brazilian desktop, every string Qt itself supplies stays English:
# the Undo / Redo / Cut / Copy / Paste / Select All menu you get on right-click
# in ANY text field, and the buttons in plain QMessageBox dialogs.
#
# qt6-translations-l10n is a Recommends of libqt6core6t64 (confirmed with
# apt-cache rdepends), and this image is built with --apt-recommends false —
# the same dropped-Recommends class as wpasupplicant, alsa-ucm-conf and rtkit.
# ki18n's startup hook reads that directory; nothing else populates it.
#
# 1.7 MB to download, 14.2 MB on disk, trixie/main. Debian does not split it per
# language, so it is all-or-nothing, and the size argument against it is weaker
# than it looks: it is .qm data and compresses roughly 8:1 into the squashfs.
#
# NOT covered by this and still English: nothing in KDE's own apps — KDE ships
# its own catalogues and those are already installed. This is Qt's layer only.
qt6-translations-l10n

# SPELL CHECK IN THE LANGUAGES THIS OS ACTUALLY SPEAKS.
#
# THE CLAIM BELOW WAS TRUE ONCE AND IS NOT TRUE OF THE SHIPPED IMAGE.
#
# It said hunspell-en-us arrives as a dependency of LibreOffice, citing
# "Setting up hunspell-en-us" in a build log. Measured in both shipped squashfs
# images: hunspell-en-us is ABSENT from free and from Pro, while hunspell-de-de,
# -fr, -es, -it and -pt-br are all present. So the product spell-checks in five
# languages and not in the one it is written in and primarily sold in.
#
# The relationship is a Recommends, not a Depends, which is why it was seen in
# one log and is missing from the image: /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/01dagric-norecommends
# and --apt-recommends false drop it, exactly as they drop the other five. A
# build log line proves a package was installed during SOME stage; only the
# squashfs proves it shipped.
#
# Pinned explicitly, like the other five, so the default language is not the one
# language left broken. This is the file's own "reads as broken, not as leaner"
# rule applied to English.
hunspell-en-us

# Nothing else does, and the
# dictionaries for other languages are Recommends, which
# /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/01dagric-norecommends drops on the image and on every
# installed machine afterwards.
#
# Dagric deliberately ships five languages: the wizard, the Hub, the helpers and
# the manual are translated into de, fr, es, pt_BR and it, and the user guide has
# a full German edition. So a German owner gets a German desktop, a German guide
# — and no red underline anywhere in Writer, in the one application people
# switch to Linux to write letters in. That reads as "the spell check is broken",
# not as "leaner".
#
# Names checked against the trixie archive rather than guessed: German and French
# both have several variants (hunspell-de-de vs -at/-ch, hunspell-fr-classical vs
# -revised), and hunspell-fr is the dependency package that pulls a real one.
# About 1-2 MB each.
hunspell-de-de
hunspell-fr
hunspell-es
hunspell-it
hunspell-pt-br

# Media — one player that plays everything
mpv

# Music library — mpv plays files, but a switcher expects a real music app
# with a library, playlists, and album art. Elisa is KDE's own: small, no
# accounts, no cloud. (Audit: free had no music library at all.)
elisa

# KOREAN, AND ONLY KOREAN, BECAUSE THAT IS THE ONLY SCRIPT ACTUALLY BROKEN.
#
# NO PERCENT SIGN MAY APPEAR ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, INCLUDING IN A COMMENT.
# live-build feeds these files through printf, so one is read as a format
# specifier: the parser errors and DROPS EVERY PACKAGE BELOW THAT LINE.
# (This paragraph cannot quote an example of the character it is about, which
# is the rule proving itself — the first draft of it tripped the guard below.)
# It is silent — no missing-package error, just a shorter list. This exact
# block cost a build: it swallowed fonts-nanum and plasma-browser-integration
# here, and gawk in system.list.chroot, and the missing gawk then killed
# 0340-offline-docs with no message at all. Same family as the inline-comment
# rule; tools/check-package-names.sh now rejects both.
#
# I very nearly put the 89 MB fonts-noto-cjk here on the assumption that free
# had no CJK coverage at all. Measured instead of assumed, by unioning every
# cmap in the resolved free font set (285 files), as a fraction of each range:
#
#     Hangul syllables  U+AC00-D7A3        3 / 11172   (essentially none)
#     CJK ideographs    U+4E00-9FFF    20902 / 20992   (all but 90)
#     Hiragana          U+3040-309F       90 / 96
#     Katakana          U+30A0-30FF       91 / 96
#
# So Chinese and Japanese already render — fonts-droid-fallback carries the
# ideographs and the kana, which is exactly what system.list.chroot claims it is
# there for. Korean does not: THREE Hangul syllables out of 11,172. And
# `fc-match :lang=ko` answers "NotoSans-Regular.ttf", a font with no Hangul in
# it at all, so nothing downstream can recover. Every Korean word on every page
# is a row of empty boxes.
#
# fonts-nanum is 33 MB and fixes precisely that. Buying the other 56 MB would
# have bought coverage that already exists, and would also have handed away a
# real Pro difference: Noto CJK stays Pro-only, where it still earns its place
# on proper Japanese and Chinese typographic forms and Serif CJK. Nanum only
# removes the boxes.
#
# Its fontconfig snippet touches only its own bold-weight metadata and does not
# rebind the sans-serif/serif/monospace aliases, so 69-dagric-lang-order.conf
# still decides the default faces.
fonts-nanum

# The Windows PowerToys "Text Extractor" of this desktop, more or less: KDE's
# own bridge between the browser and the rest of the system. Media keys control
# whatever is playing in a Firefox tab, downloads show progress in the system
# tray instead of only inside the browser, and open tabs become searchable from
# KRunner. Under 1 MB, no dependency tail, and it is KDE's own package rather
# than a third-party add-on.
#
# It needs the matching browser extension to do anything, which the owner
# installs themselves — the package alone is inert, so this is not something
# switched on behind their back.
plasma-browser-integration

# Controller support, on BOTH editions, and it contains no Valve software
# whatsoever: the package is two udev rules files and its own documentation -
# 60-steam-input.rules and 60-steam-vr.rules, 55 kB, verified by unpacking it.
# The name is the only Valve thing about it, which is why this comment exists;
# a future audit reading the never-bundle mandate would otherwise delete it.
#
# It is on free because dagric-get-steam and dagric-get-heroic live in the
# SHARED includes.chroot, so a free owner is offered Steam and Heroic with no
# udev rules behind them - the controller they plug in afterwards gets no
# permissions for its hidraw node, so Steam Input and Lutris's controller
# enumeration do not work. Plain gamepad input over evdev works without this
# (Debian 13 has hid-playstation and hid-nintendo in-tree), so the honest claim
# is about Steam Input and pairing, not about the pad being dead.
steam-devices
